ref 17592186045417] [17592186078220 #edb/ref 1069 "e-263" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078220 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the function\n${\\sqrt{2b - 3x},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 0 \\\\ -x + 2,\\end{vmatrix}x > 0$\nis continuous at $x=0$, find the value of the constant $b$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078220 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d17b0e14-aabc-5814-a242-d5fb803cb406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078220 #edb/ref 1074 "At $x=0$, the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → 0^{-})\\sqrt{2b - 3x} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2b - 3(0)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0^{+})f(x) &= lim_(x → 0^{+})(-x + 2) \\\\\n&= - (0) + 2 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0$, we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → 0^{+})f(x) \\\\\n2 &= \\sqrt{2b} \\\\\n4 &= 2b \\\\\nb &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078222 #edb/ref 1069 "e-264" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078222 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value(s) of the constant $a$ that make the function $f(x)$ below continuous at $x=2$.\n${(2x + a)^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 2 \\\\ x^{2} - 3,\\end{vmatrix}x > 2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078222 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "90e5fce3-26db-513d-9270-1e60b8dfb0d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078222 #edb/ref 1074 "At $x=2$, the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → 2^{-})(2x + a)^{2} \\\\\n&= (2(2) + a)^{2} \\\\\n&= (4 + a)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) &= lim_(x → 2^{+})(x^{2} - 3) \\\\\n&= (2)^{2} - 3 \\\\\n&= 4 - 3 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2$, we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) \\\\\n(4 + a)^{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(a + 4)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{1} \\\\\n\\mid a + 4 \\mid &= 1 \\\\\na + 4 &= ± 1 \\\\\na &= -4 ± 1 \\\\\na &= -5,-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$ for $a=-5$ or $a=-3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078225 #edb/ref 1069 "e-265" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078225 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the function\n${\\log_{2} (ax + 1),\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1 \\\\ \\log_{3} (2x + 1),\\end{vmatrix}x > 1$\nis continuous at $x=1$, find the value of $a$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078225 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b19955e9-3e5d-597e-81bc-f8f675e78e9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078225 #edb/ref 1074 "At $x=1$, the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1^{-})g(x) &= lim_(x → 1^{-})\\log_{2} (ax + 1) \\\\\n&= \\log_{2} (a(1) + 1) \\\\\n&= \\log_{2} (a + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1^{+})g(x) &= lim_(x → 1^{+})(\\log_{3} (2x + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\log_{3} (2(1) + 1) \\\\\n&= \\log_{3} (3) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1$, we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1^{-})g(x) &= lim_(x → 1^{+})g(x) \\\\\n\\log_{2} (a + 1) &= 1 \\\\\na + 1 &= 2^{1} \\\\\na &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078334 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6279" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078334 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of the constant $c$ that makes the function $h(x)$ below continuous at $x=4$.\n${4^{cx + 1},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 4 \\\\ 4x,\\end{vmatrix}x > 4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078334 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aef91e7a-138c-58bc-a25c-ca77284581c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078334 #edb/ref 1074 "At $x=4$, the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 4^{-})h(x) &= h(4) \\\\\n&= 4^{c(4) + 1} \\\\\n&= 4^{4c + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 4^{+})h(x) &= lim_(x → 4^{+})(4x) \\\\\n&= 4(4) \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=4$, we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4^{4c + 1} &= 16 \\\\\n4^{4c + 1} &= 4^{2} \\\\\n4c + 1 &= 2 \\\\\n4c &= 1 \\\\\nc &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$ for\n\n$$\nc = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078382 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078382 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078382 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078382 #edb/ref 1039 "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Radical Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078382 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078382 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "55607d46-d93c-5306-9b2e-b9a8e79366eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078383 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078383 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078383 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078383 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078383 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078383 #edb/ref 1039 "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Polynomial Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078383 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078383 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "266c7322-6395-5e9b-ad8e-fe51f3c36665" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078384 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078384 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078384 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078384 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078384 #edb/ref 1039 "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Logarithmic Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078384 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078384 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "237b4039-b516-54cb-ab4d-546578a0b1c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078490 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078490 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078490 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078490 #edb/ref 1039 "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Exponential Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078490 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078490 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e155cb07-8d89-5d4b-9015-f73300bab2e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078661 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078661 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078661 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078661 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078661 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078661 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078661 #edb/ref 1045 "Removing Jump Discontinuities" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078661 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186079227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078661 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078661 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "c69d16f5-44d0-5069-b44e-d2caf86ea506" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079012 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079012 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48583" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079012 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the function\n$g(x) = {\\sqrt{b - 3x} + 2,\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 0 \\\\ x + 3,\\end{vmatrix}x > 0$\nis continuous at $x = 0$, find the value of the constant $b$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079012 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "79180d5f-62ff-58a4-b439-cf96cc9c56ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079015 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079015 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48646" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079015 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of the constant $a$ that makes the function $g(x)$ below continuous at $x = 2$.\n$g(x) = {3^{2ax + 1},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 2 \\\\ 2x + 5,\\end{vmatrix}x > 2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079015 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e7d303c-6761-5288-b0f2-9a9708133a2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079227 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079227 #edb/ref 1061 1021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079227 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "778b28a2-a45c-54f3-993a-34eba4c8d1b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079227 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079228 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079228 #edb/ref 1061 1022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079228 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6bfe194f-fa72-57c9-8918-36cd3025c6c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079228 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079229 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079229 #edb/ref 1061 1025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079229 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "05ececba-94c0-5207-bb02-a5955fd09f40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079229 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079230 #edb/ref 1061 1027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079230 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "31a192d6-435a-5d0f-9c21-a362b860c2c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079230 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079397 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079397 #edb/ref 1061 23595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079397 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5f820e0e-c43c-5112-8821-d68f8ea15c85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079397 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079683 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:\n\n$$\n{3x^{2} + kx,\\begin{vmatrix}x < - 3, \\\\ x^{3},\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge - 3\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a jump discontinuity at $x=-3$. However, by selecting a particular value for $k$, we can remove the jump discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $k$ by forcing the left and right-sided limits of the function to be equal at $x=-3$:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to (-3)^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to (-3)^+} f(x)\n$$\n\nFor the left limit, we have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (-3)^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → (-3)^{-})(3x^{2} + kx) \\\\\n&= 3(-3)^{2} + k(-3) \\\\\n&= 27 - 3k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (-3)^{+})f(x) &= lim_(x → (-3)^{+})x^{3} \\\\\n&= (-3)^{3} \\\\\n&=-27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the left and right limits as equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n27 - 3k &= -27 \\\\\n3k &= 54 \\\\\nk &= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=-3$, we need to choose $k=18$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079683 #edb/ref 1065 543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079683 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "10dabe2c-998a-5731-bcf2-c0ed93bc7f2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079683 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
